      cat <<'HELP'
z — zoxide (smarter cd)

  z <partial>     Jump to best-matching directory
  z proj          → ~/projects (if you cd there often)
  z nvim          → ~/.config/nvim
  zi              Interactive fuzzy pick from history

  How it works:
    zoxide hooks into `cd` and records every directory you visit.
    It ranks them by "frecency" (frequency × recency). 
    It's a drop-in `cd` rplacement - you don't change any habits,
    you just type less over time.

  Install: brew install zoxide
HELP
      ;;
    downloads)
      cat <<'HELP'
downloads — jump to the Windows Downloads folder (WSL only)

  downloads

  Asks Windows where its Downloads folder actually is, rather than assuming
  /mnt/c/Users/<you>/Downloads. Two reasons that matters:

    OneDrive relocates the folder. After that, %USERPROFILE%\Downloads is
    stale or gone, and only the registry knows where it went.

    Usernames are not guessable. /mnt/c/Users here holds both `Riley` and
    `Riley Oest`; a glob picks the wrong one.

  The lookup costs ~30ms and happens once per shell, on first use — never at
  startup. To override it (unusual mount root, a Downloads folder somewhere
  else entirely), set this in ~/.bash_local:

    WIN_DOWNLOADS=/mnt/d/Downloads

  Off WSL it says so and does nothing.
HELP
      ;;
    mkcd)
      cat <<'HELP'
mkcd — create and enter directory

  mkcd my/new/dir

  Equivalent to: mkdir -p my/new/dir && cd my/new/dir
  Creates intermediate directories as needed.
HELP
      ;;
    back)
      cat <<'HELP'
back — go up N directories

  back        Go up 1 directory  (cd ..)
  back 3      Go up 3 directories (cd ../../..)

  Faster than typing ../../../ especially deep in a tree.
HELP
      ;;
    fzf)
      cat <<'HELP'
      fzf — (fuzzy everything)

  fzf <pattern>     List files matching pattern (fd search)

  Transforms three built-in keybindings:
    Ctrl+R  → fuzzy search command history (instead of reverse-i-search)
    Ctrl+T  → fuzzy file picker, inserts path at cursor
    Alt+C   → fuzzy cd into a subdirectory

  How it works: fzf reads lines from stdin (or a command), presents a
    fuzzy-searchable list, and outputs the selection. The keybindings above
    are shell integrations that pipe history/files/dirs into fzf automatically.
 
  FZF_DEFAULT_COMMAND controls what `fzf` lists when run with no input.
  We point it at `fd` so it respects .gitignore and skips .git/.

  Depends on: fzf (brew install fzf), optionally fd for faster file listing
HELP
      ;;
    extract)
      cat <<'HELP'
extract — universal archive extractor

  extract <file>

  Detects format and extracts: .tar.gz, .tar.bz2, .zip, .rar,
  .gz, .bz2, .7z, .tgz, .tbz2, .Z

  No flags to remember — just `extract whatever.tar.gz`.
HELP
      ;;
    findword)
      cat <<'HELP'
findword — whole-word grep across project

  findword <word>                     Search all files
  findword <word> -I 'dir1|dir2'     Exclude directories

  Uses ripgrep with --word-regexp (matches whole words only,
  like \b...\b). Searches hidden files, skips .git/node_modules/
  bin/obj by default.

  The -I flag takes pipe-separated directory names to exclude.

  Example:
    findword "useState" -I 'dist|coverage'

  Depends on: rg (ripgrep)
HELP
      ;;
    2*|qsv)
      cat <<'HELP'
2csv / 2txt / 2xlsx — spreadsheet conversion

  One thing to remember: "2" + the format you want. The input type is
  detected, so you never say what you're converting from.

  2csv report.xlsx             → report.csv
  2txt report.xlsx             → report.txt (tab-delimited)
  2csv legacy.xls old.xlsm     Batch: one output per input
  2csv -s "Q2 Actuals" b.xlsx  Pick a sheet by name or 0-based index
  2txt data.csv                Re-delimit an existing CSV to tabs
  2csv -f report.xlsx          Overwrite an existing output

  Reads: .xlsx .xlsm .xlsb .xls .ods .csv .tsv
  Output is named after the input, so the source is never touched.
  Refuses to clobber an existing output unless you pass -f.
  Bad input doesn't stop a batch — the rest still convert, exit is 1.

  Multi-sheet workbooks export the first sheet by default. To list them:
    qsv excel --metadata c book.xlsx

  Legacy .xls needs nothing special — calamine parses BIFF directly.

  2xlsx is the odd one out and usually unnecessary. qsv reads .xls fine
  but can't *write* xlsx, so it shells out to ssconvert, which is off by
  default: uncomment the gnumeric line in deps.conf [bash]. Reach for it
  only when you need to hand someone a real workbook — not to make a
  .xls readable, which it already is. -s doesn't apply there, since a
  workbook keeps all its sheets.

  Why qsv and not duckdb: duckdb's xlsx reader is zip-only, so .xls
  fails with "Failed to open zip for reading" — the spatial/st_read
  path fails too. It also types Excel numbers as doubles, so an id
  column comes out 1.0, 2.0 unless you pass all_varchar=true. duckdb is
  genuinely faster and lighter, and worth reaching for directly if you
  want to filter or join during conversion rather than dump a sheet.

  Re-delimiting goes through `qsv fmt`, which is CSV-aware. Don't
  replace it with tr ',' '\t' — that corrupts quoted fields.

  Depends on: qsv   (make install bash)
HELP
      ;;
    gs*|gc*|gpush*|gb*|gfetch*|gpull*)
      cat <<'HELP'
Git aliases — short forms for daily git commands

  gstatus / gs          git status
  gcommit / gc "msg"    git commit -m "msg"
  gpusho                git push origin
  gpushfo               git push --force-with-lease origin
  gchecko <br>          git checkout <branch>
  gcheckob <br>         git checkout -b <branch>
  gbranch / gb          git branch
  gbranchd / gbd        git branch -d
  gfetcho               git fetch origin
  gpullo                git pull origin

HELP
      ;;
    gprune)
      cat <<'HELP'
gprune — clean up stale local branches

  gprune

  Fetches from origin and prunes remote tracking refs, then finds
  local branches whose upstream remote has been deleted (e.g., after
  a PR merge on GitHub). Shows the list and asks for confirmation
  before deleting.

  Safe: only deletes branches whose remote is already gone.
  Uses git branch -D (force) since the remote was merged/deleted.

  Typical workflow:
    1. Merge PR on GitHub (deletes remote branch)
    2. Run `gprune` to clean up your local copy
HELP
      ;;
    gh-tui|gha)
      cat <<'HELP'
GitHub — one TUI, plus one one-shot

  gh-tui      Everything GitHub, in one interactive hub
              Pull Requests · CI/Actions · Secrets · Environments · Branches

  gha         Workflow status for HEAD — a bordered table of every
              check with its status and how long it took, plus a
              pass/fail/running summary and when the run finished
              Each check name links to its job (ctrl-click in wezterm)

  gha exists because checking CI is the thing done most often, and a
  TUI trip is the wrong price for it. Everything else about Actions —
  logs, reruns, the browser tab — is behind gh-tui's CI / Actions
  screen. gha-fail and gha-open used to live out here; they were
  `gha | grep ❌` and a URL, and neither was ever run.

  Piped or redirected, gha drops the borders and colour and emits
  plain status/name/url columns, so `gha | grep ❌` is still the
  answer gha-fail used to give.

  Press ? in any gh-tui screen for that screen's help — what each action
  does, what the preview pane shows, and the keys it accepts. That help
  used to live here, which meant reading it required already knowing to
  type `h gh-tui`, and kept it drifting out of step with the screens it
  described. It now sits next to what it documents.

  Navigation, everywhere:
    Menus:  ↑↓ nav   enter select   -/q back   ? help
    Lists:  type to filter   enter select   esc back
    Pagers: r refresh   -/q back

  The hub prefetches its preview panes once per session; press r to
  refresh them.

  Your prompt also shows CI status on the right:
    [5/5 ✅ 1d]  5 of 5 passed, last run a day ago
    [3/5 ❌ 2h]  3 of 5 passed, 2 hours ago
    [5/5 ⏳ now] still running
    [no origin]  no remote configured at all — nothing in this repo is
                 pushed anywhere. Not shown merely because a branch has
                 no upstream, which is the normal state before a first
                 push and would nag on every new branch.

  and wraps the branch itself with what GitHub says about it:
    🌍 main      the repo is public
    🔒 main      the repo is private, or internal to an org
    main 🛡️       the branch has protection rules, whether those come
                 from a ruleset or from classic branch protection

  Both are fetched in the background and cached for 5 minutes, so a repo
  you have not been in for a while shows a bare branch for one prompt and
  a decorated one from the next. Neither appears without a remote, while
  detached (a sha cannot have protection rules), or where gh cannot see
  the repo.

  Depends on: gh, fzf, jq
HELP
      ;;
    clear)
      cat <<'HELP'
clear — clear the screen, keep the scrollback

  clear              Home the cursor, erase the display
  clear -x           Same, via the real binary
  command clear      The old behaviour: also destroy the scrollback

  /usr/bin/clear sends three sequences, not two. The third is E3,
  "erase saved lines", which does not clear the screen — it discards
  the terminal's entire scrollback buffer. Windows Terminal keeps
  9001 lines by default; wezterm keeps 3500.

  Ctrl-L never did this: readline uses the terminfo `clear`
  capability, which is \E[H\E[2J with no E3. The extra sequence comes
  from the binary, not the terminal database. So the command now
  agrees with the keystroke, and emits those bytes from a shell
  builtin (~30us) rather than a fork (~500us).

  The point is your history, not the microseconds: scroll back with
  your terminal's usual keys and the output that used to be thrown
  away is still there.
HELP
      ;;
    port)
      cat <<'HELP'
port — check what's using a port

  port 3000         Show process on port 3000
  port 8080         Show process on port 8080

  Tries lsof first (works on macOS + Linux), falls back to
  ss -tlnp on Linux. Shows PID, process name, and state.
HELP
      ;;
    serve)
      cat <<'HELP'
serve — quick HTTP server

  serve              Start on port 8000
  serve 3000         Start on port 3000

  Serves the current directory via python3 -m http.server.
  Useful for quickly sharing files or testing static sites.
HELP
      ;;
    loop)
      cat <<'HELP'
loop — repeat a command (like watch)

  loop ls -la                     Refresh every 2s
  LOOP_INTERVAL=5 loop df -h     Refresh every 5s

  Clears the screen each iteration and shows a timestamp.
  Ctrl+C to stop.
HELP
      ;;
    deps|dependencies)
      # No tool list here on purpose. deps.conf is the manifest and
      # lib/manifest.sh is the only parser; a copy in this file is a second
      # manifest that drifts silently. The previous one had — it listed dotnet
      # (declared nowhere) and omitted qsv (declared), and it printed a
      # `brew install` hint on Linux.
      #
      # `make check` already answers exactly this question. Defer to it.
      if [[ -z "$DOTFILES_ROOT" ]]; then
        echo "Can't locate the dotfiles repo — ~/.bashrc isn't a symlink into it." >&2
        echo "Run 'make check' from your clone." >&2
        return 1
      fi
      make --no-print-directory -C "$DOTFILES_ROOT" check
      echo
      echo "Provenance and drift (declared vs. actual): make -C \"$DOTFILES_ROOT\" status"
      ;;
    *)
      echo "No help found for '$1'. Run 'h' to see all commands."
      ;;
  esac
}
